1:["$","$Ld",null,{"page":{"id":"library--learning-pronunciation","kind":"library-article","route":"/roadmap/library/learning-pronunciation","sourceUrl":"https://refold.la/roadmap/library/learning-pronunciation","sourceHash":"f3253fb1d8ea3d91fada7a6ec9a5a2ca0785051a45f23a72c0fee1aa0b0d76a8","collectedAt":"2026-09-01T16:53:15.261Z","bodyMode":"authorized-full-body","title":{"en":"Learning Pronunciation","zh":"学习发音"},"description":{"en":"","zh":""},"segments":[{"id":"segment-0001","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Pronunciation in the Refold method develops organically through massive input, with targeted practice layered on top in the speaking phases. You don't need to drill pronunciation from day one — in fact, too much early focus on production can cement bad habits before your ears are trained.","zh":"在 Refold 方法中，发音通过大量输入自然发展，并在口语阶段辅以针对性练习。你不必从第一天起就反复操练发音——事实上，在耳朵尚未训练好之前过早地把太多注意力放在输出上，反而可能固化错误习惯。"}},{"id":"the-progression","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"The Progression","zh":"学习进程"}},{"id":"segment-0003","type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"en":"<strong>Phase 1-2 (Input only):</strong> Your ears absorb the sounds of the language through hundreds of hours of listening. You're not required to produce anything yet, but your brain is building a mental model of how the language sounds.","zh":"<strong>第 1～2 阶段（仅输入）：</strong>你的耳朵通过数百小时的聆听吸收这门语言的声音。此时还不要求你进行任何输出，但大脑正在建立关于这门语言听起来是什么样的心理模型。"},{"en":"<strong>Phase 3 (Ear training):</strong> You sharpen your ability to distinguish sounds through ear training, intensive listening, and dialect-specific sound study. By the end of Phase 3, you have a clear mental model of how the language should sound.","zh":"<strong>第 3 阶段（听辨训练）：</strong>你通过听辨训练、精听和针对特定方言的语音学习，提升辨别声音的能力。第 3 阶段结束时，你会对这门语言应有的声音形成清晰的心理模型。"},{"en":"<strong>Phase 3D-4A (First output):</strong> Light pronunciation practice begins: chorusing, reading aloud. Your advanced listening skills act as a guide — you can hear when you're not matching the native model.","zh":"<strong>第 3D～4A 阶段（初次输出）：</strong>开始进行轻量的发音练习，例如合声模仿和朗读。你已经具备的高级听力技能会为你提供指引——当自己的发音与母语者范例不一致时，你能够听出来。"},{"en":"<strong>Phase 4B+ (Refinement):</strong> Continued practice through conversation, corrected reading aloud, and speaking analysis. Your pronunciation improves steadily with use.","zh":"<strong>第 4B 阶段及以后（精进）：</strong>继续通过对话、经纠正的朗读和口语分析进行练习。随着不断使用语言，你的发音会稳步改善。"}]},{"id":"why-ears-come-first","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Why Ears Come First","zh":"为什么听辨要先于发音"}},{"id":"segment-0005","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"When you grow up speaking a language, your brain learns to sort all the sounds you hear into a fixed set of categories — like sorting colors into bins. Sounds that are slightly different but fall into the same bin all get treated as identical.","zh":"在使用一门语言成长的过程中，大脑会学着把听到的所有声音归入一组固定类别——就像把颜色分别放进不同的格子。略有差异但落入同一个格子的声音，都会被视为完全相同。"}},{"id":"segment-0006","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"This is efficient for your native language, but it becomes a problem when learning a new one: your brain \"snaps\" unfamiliar sounds to the closest category it already knows. This is why English speakers pronounce the final vowel in \"sombrero\" as an English \"oh\" sound rather than the pure Spanish \"o\" — they're snapping to the closest vowel their brain recognizes.","zh":"这对母语来说很高效，但学习新语言时却会带来问题：大脑会把陌生的声音“吸附”到自己已知的最相近类别中。这就是为什么英语母语者会把“sombrero”末尾的元音发成英语的“oh”，而不是西班牙语纯正的“o”——他们的大脑正把它归到自己能够识别的最相近元音上。"}},{"id":"segment-0007","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Hundreds of hours of listening helps your brain start to recognize new categories instead of forcing everything through your native language filter. If you try to produce sounds before your brain can distinguish them, you'll just produce the closest native-language equivalent — and then practice that mistake over and over.","zh":"数百小时的聆听能帮助大脑开始识别新的声音类别，而不是强迫所有声音都通过母语的过滤器。如果在大脑尚不能分辨这些声音时就尝试发出来，你只会发出最接近的母语对应音——然后一遍遍练习这个错误。"}},{"id":"understanding-how-sounds-work","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Understanding How Sounds Work","zh":"理解声音的发音原理"}},{"id":"segment-0009","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"A little technical knowledge about how sounds are produced can dramatically speed up your pronunciation practice. You don't need to memorize the IPA, but knowing the basics helps you understand what to do with your mouth when a sound isn't coming out right.","zh":"了解一点声音产生方式的技术知识，可以大幅加快你的发音练习。你不必背诵国际音标，但掌握基础知识能帮助你在某个音发不对时，知道口腔应该如何调整。"}},{"id":"segment-0010","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Vowels</strong> are open, unobstructed sounds that exist on a spectrum — like colors. They're defined by three factors: how open your jaw is, how forward or back your tongue is, and how rounded your lips are. Learning a new vowel means adjusting these dimensions to hit a different point than what your mouth is used to.","zh":"<strong>元音</strong>是开放且不受阻碍的声音，存在于一个连续的光谱上——就像颜色一样。元音由三个因素决定：下颌张开的程度、舌头靠前还是靠后，以及嘴唇的圆展程度。学习一个新的元音，意味着调整这些维度，让发音落在不同于你平时习惯的点上。"}},{"id":"segment-0011","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"A useful exercise is to change one dimension at a time — for example, try saying an \"ee\" sound but with rounded lips to produce the German/French \"ü\" sound.","zh":"一个实用的练习是每次只改变一个维度——例如，尝试发“ee”音，同时把嘴唇收圆，就能发出德语或法语的“ü”音。"}},{"id":"segment-0012","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Consonants</strong> are sounds where airflow is blocked or restricted. They're more concrete than vowels — you can usually tell more clearly if you're producing one correctly. They're defined by where the blockage happens (lips, teeth, roof of mouth, throat), how it happens (full stop, friction, trill), and whether your voice box is buzzing.","zh":"<strong>辅音</strong>是气流受到阻挡或限制时产生的声音。辅音比元音更具体——通常更容易判断自己是否发得正确。辅音由阻塞发生的位置（嘴唇、牙齿、口腔顶部、喉咙）、阻塞的方式（完全闭塞、摩擦、颤音），以及声带是否振动决定。"}},{"id":"segment-0013","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"A simple test: put your hand on your throat and compare \"s\" (no buzz) with \"z\" (buzz). Knowing whether a sound should be voiced or unvoiced can instantly fix certain errors.","zh":"一个简单的测试是：把手放在喉咙上，对比“s”（不振动）和“z”（振动）。知道一个音应该是浊音还是清音，可以立刻纠正某些错误。"}},{"id":"segment-0014","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"There is a LOT more to sounds and pronunciation, but a little knowledge goes a long way.","zh":"关于声音和发音，还有非常多的知识可学，但即使只了解一点，也会大有帮助。"}},{"id":"a-progression-for-each-sound","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"A Progression for Each Sound","zh":"每个音的练习进程"}},{"id":"segment-0016","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Learning a new sound is a lot like learning to whistle — you need some knowledge of what your mouth should do, plus consistent daily practice, and eventually it clicks.","zh":"学习一个新音很像学习吹口哨——你需要了解口腔应该怎么做，坚持每天练习，最终就会豁然开朗。"}},{"id":"segment-0017","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The progression goes: learn how the sound is produced, practice it in isolation, try it in words, then work on using it in fluid speech. Being able to produce a sound correctly once doesn't mean you can use it naturally mid-sentence — that takes time.","zh":"练习进程如下：了解这个音是如何发出的，单独练习，在单词中尝试，然后练习在流畅口语中使用。偶尔正确发出一次，并不代表你能在句子中自然地使用它——这需要时间。"}},{"id":"tips","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Tips","zh":"建议"}},{"id":"segment-0019","type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"en":"Don't stress about pronunciation in the early phases. Ears first, mouth second.","zh":"在早期阶段不必为发音焦虑。先训练耳朵，再训练嘴巴。"},{"en":"If you find making sounds helpful for learning and focus, feel free to try! You're not going to damage your accent or anything, if your true focus is on the ears.","zh":"如果尝试发音有助于你的学习和专注，那就放心去试！只要真正的重点仍然放在听辨上，就不会损害你的口音或造成其他问题。"},{"en":"Chorusing is the single most effective pronunciation exercise — especially for prosody, rhythm, and tone.","zh":"合声模仿是最有效的单项发音练习——尤其有助于掌握韵律、节奏和语调。"},{"en":"Native speakers will understand you even with imperfect pronunciation. Clarity matters more than perfection.","zh":"即使发音并不完美，母语者也能听懂你。清晰比完美更重要。"},{"en":"Some sounds take lots of practice. Come back daily rather than grinding for hours in one session.","zh":"有些音需要大量练习。与其一次苦练几个小时，不如每天回来练一会儿。"},{"en":"Look up your target language's phonology on Wikipedia and compare sounds at <a href=\"https://ipachart.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ipachart.com</a>.","zh":"在 Wikipedia 上查找目标语言的音系，并前往<a href=\"https://ipachart.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ipachart.com</a>比较不同的声音。"}]},{"id":"research-and-reasoning","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Research and Reasoning","zh":"研究与理论依据"}},{"id":"segment-0021","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The \"ears first\" approach is grounded in models of L2 speech perception. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1075/lllt.17.07bes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Best &amp; Tyler (2007)</a> proposed the Perceptual Assimilation Model for L2 learners (PAM-L2), which explains how adults perceive unfamiliar sounds by assimilating them to the closest native language categories — the perceptual snapping described above. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108886901.002\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Flege &amp; Bohn (2021)</a> proposed the Speech Learning Model, which predicts that L2 sounds similar to native categories are the hardest to learn, precisely because learners keep assimilating them rather than forming new categories. Both models emphasize that accurate perception must precede accurate production.","zh":"“听辨优先”的方法以第二语言语音感知模型为基础。<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1075/lllt.17.07bes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Best &amp; Tyler（2007）</a>提出了面向第二语言学习者的知觉同化模型（PAM-L2），解释了成年人如何通过把陌生声音同化到最接近的母语类别中来感知它们——也就是上文所说的知觉“吸附”。<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108886901.002\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Flege &amp; Bohn（2021）</a>提出了语音学习模型。该模型预测，与母语类别相似的第二语言语音最难学习，原因正是学习者会不断将它们同化，而不是建立新的类别。这两个模型都强调，准确的感知必须先于准确的发音。"}},{"id":"segment-0022","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Research on perceptual training supports this. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-73606-8_9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sakai &amp; Moorman (2018)</a> reviewed High Variability Phonetic Training (HVPT) studies showing that listeners can learn to distinguish new L2 sound contrasts through intensive exposure to varied speakers, and that these perceptual gains transfer to production — even without explicit pronunciation practice.","zh":"知觉训练方面的研究支持这一点。<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-73606-8_9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sakai &amp; Moorman（2018）</a>回顾了高变异语音训练（HVPT）研究。这些研究表明，通过密集接触不同说话者的语音，听者能够学会分辨第二语言中新的语音对立；而且，即使没有进行明确的发音练习，这些知觉上的进步也能迁移到发音中。"}},{"id":"segment-0023","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The practical guidance on vowels and consonants reflects standard articulatory phonetics as described in <a href=\"https://books.google.com/books/about/A_Course_in_Phonetics.html?id=U4XaAgAAQBAJ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ladefoged &amp; Johnson (2014)</a>. A small amount of metalinguistic knowledge helps learners make targeted adjustments rather than relying on trial and error alone.","zh":"关于元音和辅音的实用指导，反映了<a href=\"https://books.google.com/books/about/A_Course_in_Phonetics.html?id=U4XaAgAAQBAJ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ladefoged &amp; Johnson（2014）</a>所描述的标准发音语音学原理。掌握少量元语言知识，可以帮助学习者进行有针对性的调整，而不是完全依赖反复试错。"}}],"cards":[]}}]
